Good timing to explore this — there are some solid open-source options depending on what you want the agent to actually do. Quick rundown:

Popular open-source agent frameworks

LangGraph (by LangChain) — best if you want fine-grained control over agent state/flow as a graph. Good for multi-step workflows, tool calling, memory. Python-first, plays well with any LLM API (including Claude).
CrewAI — higher-level abstraction, built around "crews" of agents with defined roles (like a researcher agent + writer agent). Easier to get started, less flexible for complex control flow.
AutoGen (Microsoft) — good for multi-agent conversations where agents talk to each other to solve a task. Popular for coding/research agents.
Claude Agent SDK (Anthropic's own, open-sourced) — since you're already comfortable in the Claude ecosystem, this could be the smoothest path if the agent is meant to use Claude as the backbone. Gives you tool-use, memory, and file access patterns out of the box.
smolagents (Hugging Face) — minimal, lightweight, good if you want something small and hackable rather than a heavy framework.
n8n / Flowise — if you want a more visual/no-code-ish way to wire up an agent with tools (APIs, DBs, etc.) without writing much orchestration code yourself.
How to actually set one up (typical flow, e.g. with LangGraph or Claude Agent SDK):

Pick your LLM backend (you could use the Claude API with your own key).
Define tools the agent can call — e.g., web search, a database query, a script execution, an email sender.
Define the agent loop/graph — how it decides which tool to call, how it stores intermediate state, when it stops.
Add memory/persistence if it needs to remember things across runs (SQLite, vector DB, plain JSON — depends on scale).
Run it locally first (Python venv), then containerize (Docker) if you want it scheduled/deployed.
